#{[{:activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186055867} :activity/id #uuid "542d0295-d41f-5fa8-9378-f60eaae6241d" :activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050254 :topic/id #uuid "39e988d3-6f10-5718-8fbd-1ad04a159a54" :topic/math-academy-id 48} :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186055867 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186056041 :tutorial/content "Recall that a set is **finite** if it contains a finite number of elements, while a set is **infinite** if it contains infinitely many elements.\n\nThe **cardinality** of a *finite* set $S,$ denoted $|S|,$ is the number of elements in $S.$\n\nFor example: \n\n- The set $A,$ given by\n$A = \\{ a,b,c \\}$\ncontains $3$ elements:\n\n$$\na, \\quad b, \\quad c\n$$\n\nSo, its cardinality is $|A|=3$\n\n- The set $B,$ given by\n$B = \\{2, 4, \\{ 6, 8\\}, \\emptyset\\}$\ncontains $4$ elements:\n\n$$\n2, \\quad 4, \\quad \\{6,8\\}, \\quad \\emptyset\n$$\n\nSo, its cardinality is $|B| = 4.$\n\n- The set $C,$ given by\n$C = \\{ 5, 6, 6, 7, 8, 8, 8, 9 \\}$ \ncontains $5$ *unique* elements:\n\n$$\n5, \\quad 6, \\quad 7, \\quad 8, \\quad 9\n$$\n\nSo, its cardinality $|C| = 5.$\n\nThe cardinality of a set is sometimes called its **cardinal number.** So, the cardinal numbers of $A,B,$ and $C$ are $3,4,$ and $5,$ respectively.\n\nNote that the empty set \n\n$$\n\\emptyset = \\{\\}\n$$\n\nhas cardinality $\\left|\\emptyset\\right| = 0$ since it contains no elements. The empty set is the *only* set with cardinality zero.\n\nFinally, you might be wondering about the cardinality of infinite sets. For example, what is the cardinality of the set of natural numbers?\n\n$$\n|\\mathbb N| = |\\{1,2,3,\\ldots\\}| = \\,?\n$$\n\nWhen a set is infinite, we do *not* usually write $|\\mathbb N| = \\infty.$ The reason is that although $\\mathbb N$ is infinite, so is $\\mathbb R,$ and there is a sense in which $|\\mathbb R|$ is \"larger than\" $|\\mathbb N|.$ Thus, we need an entirely new system for labeling cardinalities of infinite sets. These ideas will be discussed at length in future lessons. \n\nWe'll consider finite sets only for the remainder of this lesson." :tutorial/id #uuid "f0648f3b-bec4-5689-ac23-ac70496ecab0" :tutorial/math-academy-id 8218 :tutorial/title "Introduction"} :step/id #uuid "6f256f93-5254-5f5e-be85-a9cc0aaec093" :step/math-academy-id 65851 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186055868}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186055868 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186054824 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186054433 :question/id #uuid "cff493c6-48c0-502b-9306-9f7bc262099d" :question/math-academy-id "e-13148"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "eb35fcff-d9f0-5ae8-999e-7e8bbc0b0919" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049065}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186055077} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186055078} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186055241} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186240954} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186287687} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186287688} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186287689} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186323863} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186392024} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186392031}] :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Cardinality of a Finite Set"} :step/id #uuid "b33646a0-dc4c-5cfc-ad0b-dff69f463110" :step/math-academy-id 65852 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186055869}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186055869 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186054825 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186054434 :question/id #uuid "3dd5ad9c-c449-5072-aebb-d8980dcc0ef1" :question/math-academy-id "e-13149"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "24ce056e-1fe1-55d4-b88c-62629435003b" :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186055076} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186055083} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186055084} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186240956} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186287690} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186348380} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186348441} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186348442}] :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Cardinality of a Set With Set Elements"} :step/id #uuid "4a48db97-681